I have been estimating the below model via both ADVI and NUTS and am getting largely different answers (see below). I know that VB can often fail to work, and it's better to use MCMC. However, I am trying VB because running time is of the essence for this application.

What I am wondering explicitly here is -- since ADVI fails, does this mean that it's likely that most (typical) VB methods will also fail? I am trying to work out whether it is worth my time to calculate the VB update rules here or whether ADVI is a 'canary down the mine' signalling a general poor performance for other VB methods?

The model:

data{
  int N;
  real X1[N];
  real X2[N];
  real X3[N];
  real Y[N];
}

parameters{
  real<lower=0> beta[3];
  real alpha;
  real<lower=0> sigma;
  real beta_top;
  real<lower=0> sigma_top;
