Hi all,

First time posting here so apologies if I miss vital information/make stupid errors in what follows.

I am using ADVI (via rstan) to estimate a large heirarchical logistic regression model. The data I have is members-of-parliament-by-debate in the UK House of Commons. I have about 4m observations of whether a particular MP appeared in a particular debate, which I model as a function of MP- and debate-specific intercepts, and random effects for some predictors (the number of predictors varies according to which model I specify and is given bay the row_vector matrix X in the code below, the parameters for which are stored as Beta).

The model seems to estimate fine in that it gives me reasonable looking output from a substantive perspective, and does so pretty fast: about 30 minutes for a reasonably complex model.

However, in order to do some model selection, I wanted to make use of the `loo` package in R. I followed the advice here (https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/loo/vignettes/loo-example.html) by incorporating the log likelihood into the generated quantities block. Once this is incorporated, however, the run time slows down a lot: I'm now on hour six of waiting for the "sampling from the approximate posterior" stage to finish.

So, my question: am I doing something dumb here to make things slow down so much?

Any help would be most appreciated! ADVI and Stan are great, by the way!

Thanks,

Jack

stancode <- "

data {

int<lower = 1> N_obs; // Number of observations

int<lower = 1> N; // Number of mps

int<lower = 1> D; // Number of debates

int<lower = 1> K; // Number of topics

int nId[N_obs];  // Unique mp id

int dId[N_obs]; // Unique debate id

int Y[N_obs]; // Response

row_vector[K] X[N_obs]; // Topic proportions

real X_sig[N_obs]; // Signatures

}

parameters {

real alpha[N]; // MP effect

real delta[D]; // Debate effect

real gamma; // Signature coefficient

vector[K] Beta[N]; // MP-topic coefficients

}

model {

real mu[N_obs];

for(i in 1:N_obs)  {

mu[i] = (alpha[nId[i]] +

         delta[dId[i]] +

         dot_product(Beta[nId[i],], X[i,]) +

         gamma * X_sig[i]);

}

Y ~ bernoulli_logit(mu);

// Priors for mp intercepts

// Loop over MPs

alpha ~ normal(0, 3);

// Priors for debate intercepts

delta ~ normal(0, 3);

// Priors for topic-mp coefficients

for(k in 1:K) Beta[,k] ~ normal(0, 3);

// Prior for signature coefficients

gamma ~ normal(0, 3);

}

generated quantities {

   vector[N_obs] log_lik;

 for (n in 1:N_obs) {

 log_lik[n] = bernoulli_logit_lpmf(Y[n] | alpha[nId[n]] + delta[dId[n]] + dot_product(Beta[nId[n],], X[n,]) + gamma * X_sig[n]);

 }

 }

"

Is the reason NUTS is having a hard time due to run time and
scaling?

The model (below) is similar to the PPCA model in the ADVI paper. So some of the reasons why ADVI worked well for those models sort of applies here. Stan fits it with NUTS pretty quickly actually. The problem is that N_eff is low for some of the variables, like 3 for mu_F and 10 for lp__ out of 1000 in my last run (also rhat is above 2 for mu_F and above 1.2 for lp__). One of the best things I did when I was just playing around with NUTS was constraining B to be a unit_vector. I think this is because it helps lock down what B is in some sort of range and helps F get identified.

data {
    //sizes
    int<lower=1> T;
    int<lower=1> N;

    //data
    matrix[T, N] Y;
}
transformed data {
    real<lower=0> one_over_sqrt_N;

    one_over_sqrt_N = 1 / sqrt(N);
}
parameters {
    real mu_F;
    real<lower=0> sd_F;
    vector[T] F;
    real<lower=0> sd_B;
    unit_vector[N] B;

    real<lower=0> sd_e;
}
model {
    mu_F ~ normal(0, 0.25);
    sd_F ~ normal(0, 6);
    F ~ normal(mu_F, sd_F);
    sd_B ~ normal(0, one_over_sqrt_N);
    B ~ normal(0, sd_B);

    to_vector(Y) ~ normal(to_vector(F * B'), sd_e);

}

