> > at the end. But what if the reason you're using ADVI is because Stan has a hard time fitting the distribution? I could use this output to init a call to Stan, but are there any other options?
>
> Is the reason NUTS is having a hard time due to run time and
> scaling?
>
> The reason this recommendation is in there is that we don't know
> the conditions under which ADVI fails.  Thus we at least
> suggest testing with simulated data to make sure you're recovering
> the parameters you think you are in a simulated case.
>
> - Bob

Having 3 / 1000 effective sample per transition is hinting that NUTS
is encountering really strong correlations (i.e. your model is only
weakly identified) and you may need to add additional regularization
or reparameterize your model.

But these are exactly the problematic features that will cause ADVI
to return inaccurate results.  Remember that we have never found
a situation where ADVI returns the right answer but NUTS does not,
so when NUTS indicates an issue ADVI is especially suspect.

